CH = dict(num=13, tag="Political Science · Ch 1", theme="Belgium and Sri Lanka, why power sharing is desirable, and its forms",
 title="Power-sharing",
 intro="A democracy survives when power is shared among different groups instead of being held by a majority alone. Two contrasting stories, Belgium and Sri Lanka, show how accommodation brings peace while majority domination leads to conflict. The chapter then explains why power sharing is desirable and the forms it takes.",
 big_questions=["What happened in Belgium and Sri Lanka?", "Why is power sharing desirable?", "What are the forms of power sharing?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Belgium and Sri Lanka"),
 ("h3", "Belgium"),
 ("ul", ["A small country in Europe, with a population a little over one crore. Ethnic composition: **59 per cent** live in the **Flemish region** and speak **Dutch**; **40 per cent** live in the **Wallonia region** and speak **French**; **1 per cent** speak **German**. In the capital **Brussels**, **80 per cent** speak French and **20 per cent** Dutch.", "The French-speaking minority was rich and powerful. The Dutch-speaking community, which got the benefit of economic development and education much later, resented this. This led to tensions between the two communities, especially in Brussels.", "The Belgian leaders recognised the existence of regional differences and cultural diversities and, between **1970 and 1993**, amended the constitution four times to work out an arrangement.", "Features of the **Belgian model:** the Constitution prescribes that the number of Dutch- and French-speaking ministers shall be equal in the central government; special laws require the support of majority of members from each linguistic group; state governments are not subordinate to the central government; **Brussels** has a separate government in which both communities have equal representation; and a third kind of government, the **community government**, is elected by people belonging to one language community (Dutch, French and German-speaking) wherever they live, with power over cultural, educational and language-related issues."]),
 ("h3", "Sri Lanka"),
 ("ul", ["An island nation, just off the southern coast of Tamil Nadu; independent in **1948**. About **74 per cent** are **Sinhala**-speakers and about **18 per cent** **Tamil**-speakers: of these, 13 per cent are **Sri Lankan Tamils** and 5 per cent **Indian Tamils** (descendants of plantation workers). The rest include Christians (both Sinhala- and Tamil-speaking) and Muslims.", "In **1956** an Act was passed to recognise **Sinhala as the only official language**, disregarding Tamil. Governments followed preferential policies that favoured Sinhala applicants for university positions and government jobs; a new constitution stipulated that the state shall protect and foster **Buddhism**.", "These measures increased the feeling of alienation among Sri Lankan Tamils; their demand for **autonomy** and recognition grew into a **civil war**, beginning in the 1980s, which caused loss of thousands of lives and affected the country's social, cultural and economic life until it ended in **2009**."]),
 ("table", ["", "Belgium", "Sri Lanka"], [["Majority", "Dutch speakers (59%) but accommodated French minority", "Sinhala speakers (74%) dominated Tamil minority"], ["Approach", "Accommodation, shared power", "Majoritarianism"], ["Result", "Peace and stability", "Civil war, mistrust"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Majoritarianism** is the belief that the majority community should be able to rule a country in whichever way it wants, by disregarding the wishes and needs of the minority."),
 ("h2", "Why Power Sharing Is Desirable"),
 ("ul", ["**Prudential reasons:** power sharing helps to reduce the possibility of conflict between social groups; since social conflict often leads to violence and political instability, power sharing is a good way to ensure the stability of political order. Imposing the will of the majority in the short run may look attractive but undermines the unity of the nation in the long run.", "**Moral reasons:** power sharing is the very spirit of democracy. A democratic rule involves sharing power with those affected by its exercise, and who have to live with its effects. People have a right to be consulted on how they are to be governed; a legitimate government is one where citizens, through participation, acquire a stake in the system."]),
 ("h2", "Forms of Power Sharing"),
 ("table", ["Form", "Meaning", "Example"], [["**Horizontal** distribution", "Power is shared among different organs of government: legislature, executive and judiciary, so that none exercises unlimited power; each checks the others. This is called a **system of checks and balances**", "Ministers are answerable to the legislature; judges can review laws"], ["**Vertical** distribution", "Power shared among governments at different levels: a central government for the entire country and governments at the provincial or regional level. Also called **federal division of power**; extends to local governments", "Union, state and local governments in India"], ["Among **social groups**", "Community government in Belgium; **reserved constituencies** in legislatures and administration for socially weaker sections and women", "Reserved seats for SC and ST in India's legislatures"], ["Among **political parties, pressure groups and movements**", "Competition and coalition among parties; interest groups such as traders, industrialists, farmers and industrial workers also influence decision-making through participation in committees or exerting pressure", "Coalition governments"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "Power sharing is not a weakness. It reduces conflict, secures democracy and gives legitimacy. Belgium shows that sharing power builds unity; Sri Lanka shows that majority domination breeds strife."),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What is the ethnic composition of Belgium?", "59% Dutch (Flemish), 40% French (Wallonia), 1% German; in Brussels 80% French and 20% Dutch."),
  ("What was the 1956 Act in Sri Lanka?", "It made Sinhala the sole official language."),
  ("What is majoritarianism?", "Rule by the majority community disregarding minorities."),
  ("What is community government?", "Belgian government elected by a language community, handling cultural and educational matters."),
  ("Give two prudential reasons for power sharing.", "Reduces conflict and ensures stable political order."),
  ("What is horizontal power sharing?", "Distribution among legislature, executive and judiciary."),
  ("What is a system of checks and balances?", "Each organ of government checks the others."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Belgium **accommodated** linguistic groups; Sri Lanka's **majoritarian** policies led to civil war.", "Power sharing is desirable for **prudential** (stability) and **moral** (democratic spirit) reasons.", "Power can be shared **horizontally**, **vertically**, among **social groups** and among **parties and movements**.", "Checks and balances prevent concentration of power.", "Accommodation strengthens national unity."])
